  "prompt_text": "The figure is a timeline of early Solar System events, showing when different solid materials, planetesimals, and planets formed during roughly the first 8–9 million years after time zero. It begins with calcium-aluminum inclusions at essentially the start, over a very short interval, marking the earliest dated solids. Chondrules extend across a much longer span, with many forming before 3 million years but some continuing until about 5 million years.\n\nSuperimposed on this are stages of planetesimal and planet growth. Magmatic iron meteorite parent bodies accrete very early and then differentiate to form metallic cores within the first few million years. Mars accretion occupies an interval of a few million years, overlapping with these early planetesimal processes. Jupiter’s core is shown growing rapidly to about 20 Earth masses early in Solar System history, broadly within the same early window.\n\nThe upper part compares giant-planet system formation: the Jovian system appears to have been completed by around the mid-point of the timeline, whereas Saturn’s system could have finished later, closer to 8 million years. Overall, the figure emphasizes that rocky building blocks and differentiated planetesimals formed quickly, while giant-planet systems may have continued assembling somewhat longer.",
